<title>USA: U.S. Sept consumer prices rose 0.3 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Sept consumer prices rose 0.3 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-10-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly Consumer Price Index (CPI-U), 1982-84 equals 100:</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			  Sept    Aug   Sept'96/95</p>
<p> All Items		    0.3    0.1	3.0</p>
<p> -Excluding Food/Energy   0.3    0.1	2.7</p>
<p> Energy			Unch   -0.6	5.2</p>
<p> Food, Drink		  0.5    0.3	3.7</p>
<p> Food			   0.5    0.4	3.8</p>
<p> CPI-Urban Consumers-X  157.8  157.3	N/A</p>
<p>X-Data unadjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			  Sept    Aug   Sept'96/95</p>
<p> Services		     0.2    0.2	3.3</p>
<p> Housing			0.2    0.2	2.9</p>
<p> -Home Fuel Oil	     3.6    0.5     10.4</p>
<p> -Homeowner Costs-Y	 0.2    0.2	3.0</p>
<p> Apparel, Upkeep	    0.5   -1.4     -0.9</p>
<p> Transportation	     0.5   -0.2	3.2</p>
<p> -Gasoline		   -0.4   -2.0	6.0</p>
<p> -New Cars		    0.5    0.3	2.3</p>
<p>Y-Dec 1982=100 base.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			  Sept    Aug   Sept'96/95</p>
<p> Medical Care		 0.2    0.2	3.3</p>
<p> Commodities		  0.4   -0.1	2.6</p>
<p> CPI-W			  0.3    0.1	3.0</p>
<p>CPI-W is the Consumer Price Index for urban wage earners and clerical workers.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.3 percent rise in September consumer prices. Excluding food and energy, they also expected a 0.3 percent increase.</p>
<p>The department said airline fares rose 2.9 percent in September, the biggest gain since a 5.9 percent jump in February.</p>
<p>It also said new vehicles prices climbed 0.6 percent in September, posting the biggest gain since a similar increase in January 1991.</p>
